.TH GLAZE-SERVICE 1 "2026-11-10" "Glaze 0.1.0" "Glaze User Commands"
.SH NAME
glaze-service \- Glaze application registry, launcher and local IPC service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glaze-service
.br
.B glaze-service
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glaze-service
is the daemon that owns Glaze's application data: it discovers and validates
real XDG
.B .desktop
entries, ranks launcher search over that registry, and launches applications
safely. The GTK shell does not read
.B .desktop
files itself; it talks to this service over a local Unix socket and receives
validated data (see
.BR glaze (1)
for the client side).
.PP
.B IMPLEMENTATION STATUS:
the service library
.RB ( glaze-service )
exists and is unit-tested, but there is no daemon binary yet. The crate is
built as a library only; a
.B glaze-service
executable and its option parsing are being added by the system-integration
workstream. Until then, there is no running daemon for the shell or the
.B glaze
client to connect to, and
.B glaze-session check
reports the component as missing.
.SH ROLE (IMPLEMENTED AS A LIBRARY)
The library provides, and its tests cover:
.TP
.B Application registry
Discovers real
.B .desktop
files from the XDG data directories in precedence order, applies the standard
visibility rules
.RB ( Hidden ,
.BR NoDisplay ,
.BR OnlyShowIn ,
.BR NotShowIn ,
.BR TryExec )
and de-duplicates by desktop-file id so a user entry shadows a system entry.
.TP
.B Desktop-entry parsing
Parses groups, localised keys, string lists and
.B Exec
tokenisation with field codes, and proves that shell metacharacters in that data
stay inert (they are never handed to a shell).
.TP
.B Launcher search
Deterministic, documented ranking
.RB ( exact " > " prefix " > " word-prefix " > " substring " > " subsequence ,
plus keyword, generic-name, comment and desktop-id contributions and a length
tiebreak) with match indices for highlighting. Every hit is a real registered
application.
.TP
.B Safe launch
Builds an argv vector, detaches the child and wraps
.B Terminal=true
applications in an available terminal emulator. It never invokes a shell.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET AND PROTOCOL
When the daemon is implemented it will serve newline-delimited JSON over a Unix
stream socket at
.RB $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/glaze/control.sock
with mode
.BR 0600 .
One request line produces one response line. The protocol is versioned; a
mismatch is reported as an error rather than ignored. The request set is:
.BR hello ", " ping ", " list-apps ", " launch-app ", " search-apps ,
.BR list-windows ", " list-workspaces ", " activate-window ,
.BR close-window ", " reload " and " status .
Window and workspace requests return an empty list or a
.B backend-unavailable
error when no compositor backend is attached; nothing is fabricated.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage. (To be provided by the daemon binary when it lands.)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Base directory for the control socket.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_DIRS
Application-directory search path.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ", " LANG
Used for
.B OnlyShowIn
and
.B NotShowIn
matching and localised names.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown. Not reachable until the daemon binary exists.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR glaze (1),
.BR glaze-session (1),
.BR glaze-shell (1),
.BR glaze-settings (1),
.BR glaze-compositor (1)
